  "answer": "It depends on what you mean by “left out”.\n\n**Deleting it from the mark:** the supplied passages do not explain whether removing a name or symbol by amendment is permitted. I cannot say what would be allowed or decided for your particular application.\n\n**Ignoring it when assessing confusion:** section 43 of the Act expressly covers a connotation arising from either the trade mark or a sign contained in it. If that connotation makes use of the mark likely to deceive or cause confusion for the relevant goods or services, the statutory consequence is rejection of the application—not simply disregarding the sign [TMA1995/s43].\n\n**Manual practice:** a name’s mere presence does not automatically establish a section 43 objection [TMM/Part29/3#1~1]. The Manual requires a real and obvious danger of deception or confusion arising from the mark’s secondary meaning; a mere possibility is insufficient [TMM/Part29/3/3/1~1]. These are statements of examination practice, distinct from the Act’s requirement."
